    faqs: [
        { title: 'What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?', answer: 'Supervised learning uses labeled data (input-output pairs) to learn a mapping function. Unsupervised learning finds patterns in unlabeled data. Examples: classification/regression (supervised) vs clustering/dimensionality reduction (unsupervised).' },
        { title: 'How do I choose between different ML algorithms?', answer: 'Consider: data size (deep learning needs more), feature types (tabular: tree-based; images: CNN; text: transformers), interpretability needs, training time constraints, and whether the problem is classification, regression, or clustering.' },
        { title: 'What is overfitting and how do I prevent it?', answer: 'Overfitting occurs when a model memorizes training data instead of learning general patterns. Prevention: more training data, regularization (L1/L2), dropout, early stopping, cross-validation, data augmentation, and simpler models.' },
        { title: 'How does backpropagation work?', answer: 'Backpropagation computes gradients of the loss function with respect to each weight by applying the chain rule backwards through the network. It enables efficient gradient computation for training neural networks with gradient descent.' },
        { title: 'What is the bias-variance tradeoff?', answer: 'Bias is error from wrong assumptions (underfitting). Variance is error from sensitivity to training data (overfitting). Total error = bias^2 + variance + irreducible error. Complex models have low bias but high variance, and vice versa.' },
        { title: 'When should I use deep learning vs traditional ML?', answer: 'Use deep learning for: large datasets, unstructured data (images, text, audio), complex patterns. Use traditional ML for: small datasets, tabular data, when interpretability matters, limited compute resources, or when feature engineering expertise is available.' },
        { title: 'How do I handle imbalanced datasets?', answer: 'Techniques: oversampling minority class (SMOTE), undersampling majority class, class weights in loss function, ensemble methods (balanced random forest), threshold adjustment, use F1/AUC instead of accuracy, and collect more minority class data.' },
        { title: 'What is transfer learning?', answer: 'Transfer learning reuses a model trained on one task as the starting point for another related task. Common in computer vision (ImageNet pre-trained CNNs) and NLP (pre-trained transformers like BERT). It reduces data and compute requirements significantly.' },
        { title: 'How do I evaluate a classification model?', answer: 'Use precision (correctness of positive predictions), recall (coverage of actual positives), F1-score (harmonic mean), ROC-AUC (ranking quality), and confusion matrix. Choose metrics based on the cost of false positives vs false negatives.' },
        { title: 'What is the vanishing gradient problem?', answer: 'In deep networks, gradients can shrink exponentially as they propagate backward through layers with sigmoid/tanh activations. This prevents early layers from learning. Solutions: ReLU activation, batch normalization, residual connections, and proper initialization.' },
        { title: 'How does a convolutional neural network work?', answer: 'CNNs use convolutional layers with learnable filters that slide across inputs to detect patterns. Pooling layers reduce dimensions. Deeper layers detect increasingly abstract features. The architecture exploits spatial structure for efficient learning.' },
        { title: 'What is gradient descent and its variants?', answer: 'Gradient descent updates parameters in the direction of steepest descent. Batch GD uses all data (stable but slow). SGD uses one sample (noisy but fast). Mini-batch GD balances both. Adam combines momentum and adaptive learning rates.' },
        { title: 'How do random forests reduce overfitting?', answer: 'Random forests train multiple decision trees on bootstrap samples (bagging) with random feature subsets at each split. Averaging predictions reduces variance while maintaining low bias. Each tree overfits differently, and averaging cancels out the noise.' },
        { title: 'What are word embeddings?', answer: 'Word embeddings are dense vector representations of words where similar words have similar vectors. Word2Vec, GloVe, and FastText learn static embeddings. BERT and GPT produce contextual embeddings that vary based on surrounding context.' },
        { title: 'How do I handle missing data in ML?', answer: 'Options: drop rows/columns (if few missing), impute with mean/median/mode, KNN imputation, model-based imputation (MICE), or use algorithms that handle missing values natively (XGBoost). Always consider why data is missing (MCAR, MAR, MNAR).' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularization?', answer: 'L1 (Lasso) adds |w| penalty, producing sparse models (feature selection). L2 (Ridge) adds w^2 penalty, shrinking all weights toward zero. L1 can zero out features; L2 distributes weight across all features. Elastic Net combines both.' },
        { title: 'How do GANs work?', answer: 'GANs train two networks adversarially: a generator creates fake samples to fool the discriminator, which tries to distinguish real from fake. Training alternates between the two. When balanced, the generator produces realistic samples.' },
        { title: 'What is cross-validation and why is it important?', answer: 'Cross-validation estimates model performance on unseen data by splitting data into train/test folds. K-fold CV uses each fold as test once. It prevents overfitting to a single train/test split and gives a more reliable performance estimate.' },
        { title: 'How does the transformer attention mechanism work?', answer: 'Self-attention computes relevance scores between all position pairs. Query, Key, Value matrices are derived from inputs. Attention weights = softmax(QK^T/sqrt(d)). Multi-head attention runs parallel attention computations. This captures long-range dependencies without recurrence.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between precision and recall?', answer: 'Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of all positive predictions, how many are correct. Recall = TP/(TP+FN): of all actual positives, how many are found. High precision means few false alarms. High recall means few missed positives. The tradeoff depends on application costs.' },
    ],
